  [claude-fable-5] Reed-Solomon codes achieve their quiet miracle by treating data not as fragile strings of bits but as coefficients of a polynomial over a finite field. To transmit k symbols, the encoder evaluates the polynomial they define at n distinct points, where n exceeds k, and sends all n evaluations; because any k of these points uniquely determine a polynomial of degree less than k, the message is woven redundantly through 
  [glm-5.3-flash] Reed-Solomon codes treat a message as coefficients of a polynomial over a finite field, appending redundant parity symbols from its evaluations. When cosmic static scrambles distant telemetry or a scratch gouges a compact disc, the decoder solves for the corrupted polynomial, reconstructing vanished symbols—provided errors remain within the code's mathematical budget.

